WEBVTT

00:06.700 --> 00:08.601
It was awfully nice of our
new neighbors

00:08.635 --> 00:10.102
to invite us
over for dinner.

00:10.137 --> 00:11.037
I don't know, Lois.

00:11.071 --> 00:12.305
Going to
Cleveland's house

00:12.339 --> 00:13.923
with someone else
living there just seems weird.

00:13.957 --> 00:16.609
Like when a new Pope
moves into the Vatican.

00:16.643 --> 00:18.577
Hi, I don't know
who to complain to about this,

00:18.612 --> 00:21.614
but I'm still getting
XVIII's mail.

00:21.648 --> 00:22.848
Yeah, no.

00:22.883 --> 00:25.384
No, he's not here anymore,
this is XXIV.

00:25.419 --> 00:27.219
<i>Yeah, no, I don't need
Newsweek.</i>

00:27.254 --> 00:29.555
<i>No, I don't need
National Geographic.</i>

00:29.589 --> 00:32.858
<i>Oh yeah, no, yeah, we'll-we'll
hang on to Boys' Life.</i>

00:33.894 --> 00:35.778
Hello, neighbors.

00:35.812 --> 00:37.313
Joe, you can walk.

00:37.347 --> 00:38.314
How do you do?

00:38.348 --> 00:39.348
I'm Stan Smith.

00:39.383 --> 00:40.499
Please, come in.

00:42.669 --> 00:45.338
Oh, I'm so glad
everyone could make it.

00:45.372 --> 00:46.772
Stan, I need $50.

00:46.807 --> 00:50.242
They're selling a biopsy of
Celine Dion's uterus on eBay.

00:50.277 --> 00:51.711
What the hell
kind of dog is that?

00:51.745 --> 00:53.946
I was going to
ask the same thing.

00:53.980 --> 00:55.898
Oh, uh, that's
our alien, Roger.

00:55.932 --> 00:57.850
He lives in our attic,
but nobody can know.

00:57.884 --> 01:00.036
And don't tell
anybody about our attic.

01:00.070 --> 01:01.037
(phone ringing)

01:01.071 --> 01:02.038
Hello?

01:02.072 --> 01:03.172
Oh, hey, Quagmire.

01:03.206 --> 01:04.590
Hey, you're not gonna
believe this.

01:04.624 --> 01:06.776
I'm over here with Joe,
and he's got a space alien.

01:07.961 --> 01:09.478
(all screaming)

01:09.513 --> 01:11.547
(moaning)

01:13.650 --> 01:14.633
Peter, wake up.

01:14.668 --> 01:16.068
You're having
a nightmare.

01:16.103 --> 01:17.069
(gasps)

01:17.104 --> 01:18.070
Oh, Lois.

01:18.105 --> 01:19.755
Thank God,
it was just a dream.

01:19.790 --> 01:20.906
Hey.

01:20.941 --> 01:23.309
Lois, what's that
fat man doing in our bed?

01:23.343 --> 01:26.479
Ugh, damn it,
I always wake up

01:26.513 --> 01:29.548
before I find out if they can
understand the baby.

01:29.583 --> 01:33.252
<i>( King of the Hill
theme song playing)</i>

02:05.435 --> 02:07.803
Thanks for helping me set
up this jungle gym, guys.

02:07.838 --> 02:09.905
Lois says Stewie's been
getting a little chunky.

02:09.940 --> 02:12.591
Ugh, everybody's
always commenting on my weight.

02:12.626 --> 02:14.877
This is why
I almost died from anorexia.

02:14.911 --> 02:17.646
(monitor beeping)

02:17.681 --> 02:18.948
You...

02:18.982 --> 02:20.382
look...

02:20.417 --> 02:21.617
fat.

02:21.651 --> 02:23.786
All right, I got the
monkey bars all put together.

02:23.820 --> 02:25.037
Aw, God,
look at that,

02:25.071 --> 02:26.756
they already got
Al Qaedas all over them.

02:26.790 --> 02:27.973
Get, get
out of here.

02:27.991 --> 02:29.191
Scat!

02:29.226 --> 02:30.810
Man, they're gonna be so good

02:30.844 --> 02:32.795
at swinging bar-to-bar,
when they invade.

02:32.829 --> 02:35.781
Wow, Quagmire, how did you
build that part so fast?

02:35.816 --> 02:36.849
It was easy.

02:36.883 --> 02:38.033
I just followed
the instructions.

02:38.084 --> 02:39.364
I couldn't find
the English ones.

02:39.369 --> 02:40.920
These are in Latin
and those stuffy

02:40.954 --> 02:42.788
Ivy League professors
won't help me.

02:42.823 --> 02:44.323
(laughing)

02:44.357 --> 02:46.142
<i>Nullum gratuitum
prandium, eh, Peter?</i>

02:46.176 --> 02:47.376
Oh, yeah?

02:47.410 --> 02:49.050
Why don't you catch
a football, you nerds?

02:50.113 --> 02:51.113
Nerds.

02:51.148 --> 02:52.331
No, I just read the back,

02:52.349 --> 02:53.382
where it's printed in French.

02:53.433 --> 02:54.733
You speak French?

02:54.768 --> 02:56.235
Sure, they love mein Fran.

02:57.938 --> 03:00.523
(French accent): Oh, Quagmire!
Shh. Quagmire!

03:00.557 --> 03:02.224
<i>Silence!
C'est Quagmire.</i>

03:05.695 --> 03:08.531
(audience laughing)

03:09.649 --> 03:11.867
Quagmire.
Aw, Quagmire.

03:11.902 --> 03:14.687
Oh, Quagmire, don't
fall near ze mouse trap.

03:14.721 --> 03:16.372
Yeah, and French also
comes in handy

03:16.406 --> 03:18.007
when I have layovers
in Montreal.

03:18.041 --> 03:19.275
That place is the best.

03:19.309 --> 03:20.976
I always wanted to go
to Canada,

03:21.011 --> 03:22.995
<i>but then South Park went,
so we couldn't go.</i>

03:23.029 --> 03:24.880
Oh, man, you guys should
come with me sometime.

03:24.915 --> 03:26.756
Montreal has the best
strip clubs in the world.

03:26.783 --> 03:27.766
They do?

03:27.801 --> 03:29.001
Yeah, they're unbelievable.

03:29.035 --> 03:30.870
The girls
up in Canada are gorgeous.

03:30.904 --> 03:32.087
And they
all play ice hockey,

03:32.122 --> 03:33.739
so they lose
their teeth by age ten.

03:33.773 --> 03:34.940
Wow.

03:34.975 --> 03:36.742
In fact,
I'd even go so far as to say

03:36.776 --> 03:38.010
that a Canadian strip club

03:38.044 --> 03:40.379
is the most magical place
in the world.

03:43.583 --> 03:45.634
♪ Nip nip here, nip nip there

03:45.669 --> 03:47.386
♪ And a bevy of porno stars

03:47.404 --> 03:49.605
♪ There's jugand mugs of
Molson beer ♪

03:49.656 --> 03:51.724
♪ In Canadian nudie bars

03:51.791 --> 03:53.792
♪ Dirty ho's put on shows

03:53.827 --> 03:55.578
♪ And a lot
of 'em don't have scars ♪

03:57.747 --> 03:59.315
♪ In Canadian nudie bars

03:59.349 --> 04:01.183
♪ You can touch
and squeeze 'em ♪

04:01.218 --> 04:03.485
♪ They don't even care

04:03.520 --> 04:05.638
♪ Would they even dye
their pubes ♪

04:05.672 --> 04:06.355
♪ To match my hair?

04:06.389 --> 04:07.289
♪ Uh-huh

04:07.324 --> 04:08.557
♪ Color me there

04:08.592 --> 04:10.125
♪ Like me, say "Oui-Oui"

04:10.160 --> 04:12.111
♪ And tell them you're a friend
of Bill Maher's ♪

04:12.145 --> 04:13.963
♪ That's how you'll get
to watch them pee ♪

04:13.997 --> 04:15.831
♪ In Canadian nudie bars

04:15.866 --> 04:17.266
♪ Ha, ha, ha

04:17.300 --> 04:18.367
♪ Ho, ho, ho

04:18.401 --> 04:19.268
BOTH:
♪ Ha, ha, ha, ha

04:19.302 --> 04:20.469
♪ Ha

04:20.520 --> 04:22.137
♪ A guy can get
his hockey pucked ♪

04:22.172 --> 04:25.841
♪ In Canadian nudie bars.

04:25.876 --> 04:27.209
That sounds incredible!

04:27.244 --> 04:28.227
We got to go.

04:28.261 --> 04:29.261
Yeah, will you
take us, Quagmire?

04:29.296 --> 04:30.246
Please?

04:30.280 --> 04:31.313
All right,
let's do it!

04:31.364 --> 04:32.314
But I don't want to
get any crap

04:32.349 --> 04:33.399
from your wives about me

04:33.433 --> 04:34.333
taking you to a strip joint.

04:34.367 --> 04:35.527
So you guys are gonna have to

04:35.535 --> 04:36.656
lie about where we're going.

04:36.670 --> 04:38.153
Okay, sure.
Yeah, no problem.

04:38.204 --> 04:39.321
I'm a great liar.

04:41.708 --> 04:43.208
Okay, we're
good this way.

04:43.243 --> 04:44.743
Is it clear
in your direction?

04:47.047 --> 04:48.430
Yep.

04:51.635 --> 04:53.936
<i>HOST: We now return to
Stabbed in the Butt,</i>

04:53.970 --> 04:56.205
the only game show where if
you get the answer wrong,

04:56.239 --> 04:57.439
you get
stabbed in the butt.

04:57.474 --> 04:59.775
All right, Bradley,
here's your question.

04:59.809 --> 05:02.378
What is the
atomic weight of nitrogen?

05:04.180 --> 05:04.980
Get him, guys!

05:05.015 --> 05:06.348
Stab him
in the butt!

05:06.399 --> 05:07.850
Okay,
bye, you guys.

05:07.884 --> 05:09.118
I'm leaving now.

05:09.152 --> 05:11.353
Peter, tell me again
why you're going to Montreal?

05:11.388 --> 05:13.105
Hey, do I give you
the third degree

05:13.139 --> 05:15.608
every time you go for one of
those luxurious mammograms?

05:15.642 --> 05:17.576
Dad, why can't we all go?

05:17.611 --> 05:18.979
Because somebody's
got to stay here

05:19.013 --> 05:20.313
and round up all
these rattlesnakes.

05:20.348 --> 05:21.298
(rattling)

05:21.332 --> 05:22.299
(all screaming)

05:22.333 --> 05:23.333
Bye.

05:27.922 --> 05:29.172
Hey, guys.

05:29.206 --> 05:30.790
Check it out.
What do you think?

05:30.824 --> 05:32.042
Wow, a private plane?

05:32.093 --> 05:33.610
How'd you swing
that, Quagmire?

05:33.644 --> 05:35.946
Well, let's just say
I walked in on John Travolta

05:35.980 --> 05:37.614
with not Kelly Preston.

05:37.648 --> 05:40.583
Oh, my God,
this plane is awesome, Quagmire.

05:40.618 --> 05:41.985
Hey, who's that?

05:42.019 --> 05:43.753
Oh, that's
J. Private Planington.

05:43.788 --> 05:45.221
He invented
private planes.

05:45.256 --> 05:47.624
He was also the world's
foremost squid hunter.

05:47.658 --> 05:50.260
I've got a lot of big
ideas, too,

05:50.294 --> 05:51.795
but everybody's
just so jealous.

05:53.848 --> 05:55.682
Boy, this is nice.

05:55.716 --> 05:57.434
Hey, Joe, I'm gonna
go check out the john.

05:59.720 --> 06:01.421
Oh, my God,
have you seen this?

06:01.455 --> 06:02.989
Look how fancy this thing is.

06:03.024 --> 06:05.008
They even got a special map
that lets you choose

06:05.042 --> 06:06.676
who you wan to dump
your poop on.

06:08.379 --> 06:10.947
Geez, the synagogue
button's almost worn out.

06:14.385 --> 06:16.419
Oh, my God, Joe,
there's a man on the wing.

06:16.470 --> 06:18.054
Peter, there's
no man on the wing.

06:18.089 --> 06:19.289
There is! I saw him!

06:19.323 --> 06:20.991
Peter, stop
being ridiculous.

06:21.025 --> 06:22.225
I'm telling you,
lift up the shade.

06:22.259 --> 06:23.159
There's a man on the wing.

06:23.194 --> 06:25.028
(sighs) Fine.

06:25.062 --> 06:26.096
It's me.

06:26.130 --> 06:27.263
I'm the
man on the wing.

06:27.298 --> 06:29.032
Peter, how the hell
did you get out there?

06:29.066 --> 06:30.350
Are you crazy?!

06:30.384 --> 06:31.818
Get back
in here, Peter!

06:33.054 --> 06:34.587
What the hell's going on
back there?

06:34.622 --> 06:35.622
We're losing altitude.

06:38.192 --> 06:39.726
Hang on, you guys;
I don't know

06:39.760 --> 06:41.227
if I can pull out
of this-- giggity!

06:41.262 --> 06:43.163
Wow, from up here,
everything looks

06:43.197 --> 06:44.864
so huge and coming faster.

06:58.546 --> 07:00.980
Uh, what happened?

07:01.015 --> 07:02.148
Did we crash?

07:02.183 --> 07:03.717
Yeah, we crashed.

07:03.751 --> 07:04.918
Wow.

07:04.952 --> 07:06.720
Still, you know,
we saved so much trouble

07:06.754 --> 07:08.688
not flying commercial,
I think we came out ahead.

07:08.723 --> 07:09.856
Sure.

07:09.890 --> 07:11.424
Drive right up to the plane.
Exactly.

07:11.459 --> 07:13.460
If we went to Logan,
that plane wouldn't crash

07:13.494 --> 07:14.761
for another hour,
at least.

07:17.131 --> 07:18.465
Oh, they made
pretty good time.

07:24.833 --> 07:26.192
(sighs) I'm bored.

07:26.193 --> 07:28.361
Peter, you made me crash
the damn plane!

07:28.395 --> 07:30.029
Okay, I know you're a
pilot and everything,

07:30.064 --> 07:31.898
but not everyone
likes to talk about planes

07:31.932 --> 07:33.333
as much as you, Quagmire.

07:34.401 --> 07:36.169
Well, we're out
of cell phone range

07:36.203 --> 07:37.043
and the radio's shot.

07:37.071 --> 07:38.171
This is not good.

07:38.205 --> 07:39.339
No one knows
we're out here,

07:39.373 --> 07:40.974
and we've got
no food or water.

07:41.008 --> 07:42.909
Relax, Quagmire,
there's food all around us.

07:42.943 --> 07:44.177
The trick is just knowing

07:44.211 --> 07:45.812
what's safe to eat
and what's not.

07:50.851 --> 07:52.185
Okay, these are a "maybe."

07:52.219 --> 07:54.037
You guys, I think,
for the time being,

07:54.071 --> 07:56.339
we're gonna have to start
thinking in terms of survival.

07:56.373 --> 07:57.807
Yeah, we're just
gonna have to do

07:57.841 --> 07:59.142
what Davy Crockett
would do.

07:59.176 --> 08:01.327
I mean, that guy killed a
bear when he was only three.

08:01.395 --> 08:03.096
Honey, don't you
think it's weird

08:03.130 --> 08:04.297
that our
three-year-old son

08:04.331 --> 08:05.431
is murdering animals?

08:05.466 --> 08:06.533
Nonsense.

08:06.567 --> 08:08.017
He's a healthy,
normal young boy.

08:08.051 --> 08:11.187
Ma, Pa, I
killed my pet raccoon.

08:11.221 --> 08:12.839
Why, Davy?

08:12.873 --> 08:14.340
So I could
have this cool hat

08:14.375 --> 08:16.910
and 'cause it felt like
heaven when I was doing it.

08:22.883 --> 08:24.284
Well, it's getting dark.

08:24.318 --> 08:25.785
We should
probably start a fire.

08:25.819 --> 08:27.020
Man, how do
we do that?

08:27.054 --> 08:28.404
Hey, do you think
there's directions

08:28.439 --> 08:30.039
about starting
fires in the song

08:30.074 --> 08:31.274
"We Didn't
Start the Fire"?

08:31.308 --> 08:32.275
I don't think so.

08:32.309 --> 08:33.509
I better go
through it all.

08:33.544 --> 08:35.278
♪ Stuff, stuff,
stuff and stuff ♪

08:35.312 --> 08:36.913
♪ History and stuff and stuff

08:36.947 --> 08:39.515
♪ People, people, someone's
name, history and sports ♪

08:39.550 --> 08:41.351
♪ Big disaster, someone's name

08:41.385 --> 08:43.253
♪ Stuff and stuff
and stuff and stuff ♪

08:43.287 --> 08:44.487
♪ History, someone's name

08:44.521 --> 08:45.722
♪ Something I don't know

08:45.756 --> 08:47.423
♪ Famous guy, movie star

08:47.458 --> 08:48.891
♪ Don't know who
these people are ♪

08:48.926 --> 08:50.326
♪ Stuff and stuff and history

08:50.361 --> 08:52.061
♪ Yelling really loud at me!

08:52.096 --> 08:53.429
Okay, how's
the fire coming?

08:53.464 --> 08:54.624
(frogs croaking, owl hooting)

08:56.900 --> 08:58.351
Okay, this should work.

08:58.385 --> 08:59.752
If it rains, at least
we know we can stay dry.

08:59.787 --> 09:00.753
Yeah.

09:00.788 --> 09:02.055
So, now our number one concern

09:02.089 --> 09:04.290
is that Predators
are watching our body heat.

09:04.325 --> 09:05.458
Peter, that's
just a movie.

09:05.492 --> 09:07.527
That's not real.

09:07.561 --> 09:09.128
(distorted): No, I think we
need to cover ourselves in mud

09:09.163 --> 09:10.163
to avoid being seen.

09:10.197 --> 09:11.331
Peter, Joe's right.

09:11.365 --> 09:12.465
That's
only a movie.

09:12.499 --> 09:13.866
Oh, God,
somebody farted!

09:13.901 --> 09:14.968
Ugh, it stinks!

09:15.002 --> 09:16.419
We should not rule out
the Predator

09:16.453 --> 09:17.453
having been the farter.

09:20.591 --> 09:21.912
Okay, we still
got nothing to eat,

09:21.925 --> 09:23.025
so it looks
like we're gonna

09:23.043 --> 09:24.744
have to hunt
animals for food.

09:24.778 --> 09:26.179
And since we
have no weapons,

09:26.213 --> 09:28.081
I set up some
traps and snares.

09:28.115 --> 09:29.499
Well, I was about to
take a leak.

09:29.533 --> 09:30.633
Can I go
in that direction?

09:30.668 --> 09:31.534
Yes.

09:31.552 --> 09:32.835
(screaming, cracking)
No.

09:32.870 --> 09:34.404
Ah, damn it, my legs!

09:34.438 --> 09:35.505
I think they're broken!

09:35.539 --> 09:36.673
Yay, Joe.

09:36.707 --> 09:38.308
Now you have a friend.

09:41.345 --> 09:43.313
Damn it, Peter,
this is all your fault.

09:43.347 --> 09:45.181
Listen, I wish
you had a family

09:45.215 --> 09:48.101
to take care of you, too,
but that's a choice you made.

09:48.152 --> 09:49.268
Here you go, guys.

09:49.303 --> 09:52.288
First time in a while
I've had wood in my lap.

09:52.323 --> 09:54.624
Are... are we supposed
to laugh at that?

09:54.658 --> 09:55.538
'Cause it's upsetting.

09:55.559 --> 09:57.193
Man, being
out here sucks.

09:57.227 --> 09:58.945
I mean,
I'm not a forest guy.

09:58.979 --> 10:00.046
I-I'm more of a

10:00.080 --> 10:01.614
"take-it-all-in-
at-the-beach" guy.

10:01.649 --> 10:04.484
(sighs contentedly)

10:04.518 --> 10:05.702
Excuse me, sir.

10:05.736 --> 10:06.753
This is
private property.

10:06.787 --> 10:07.804
what are you doing?

10:07.838 --> 10:08.721
(sighs)

10:08.756 --> 10:10.056
Just taking it all in.

10:10.090 --> 10:12.375
You're staring
at my daughter's bedroom.

10:12.409 --> 10:14.243
She's really
grown into her body.

10:14.278 --> 10:16.145
This is gonna
be a fun summer for her.

10:16.180 --> 10:18.364
You guys, we've been out here
a couple days now,

10:18.399 --> 10:21.217
and I don't think
anyone's coming to get us.

10:21.251 --> 10:23.136
It might be time
for one of us to go for help.

10:23.170 --> 10:24.537
Well, what do
you want us to do?

10:24.571 --> 10:26.272
Peter's the
only one who can walk.

10:26.306 --> 10:27.390
What?!

10:27.424 --> 10:28.591
You want me to
just walk out there,

10:28.625 --> 10:29.759
straight into the woods?

10:29.777 --> 10:31.094
Peter, if you don't,

10:31.128 --> 10:33.763
the only other option
is we sit here and wait to die.

10:33.797 --> 10:36.065
(sighs)
All right, I'll do it.

10:36.100 --> 10:37.250
If I just keep walking,

10:37.284 --> 10:38.368
I gotta hit civilization

10:38.402 --> 10:39.369
at some point, right?

10:39.403 --> 10:40.403
Thanks, Peter.

10:40.437 --> 10:41.404
Yeah.

10:41.438 --> 10:43.406
And good luck.

10:45.042 --> 10:46.609
Uh-oh.

10:46.643 --> 10:47.744
What?

10:47.778 --> 10:50.046
Well, I hate to ask
you this, Quagmire,

10:50.080 --> 10:51.647
but do you know how
to change a diaper?

10:51.682 --> 10:53.282
Yeah, of course I know
how to change a diaper.

10:53.317 --> 10:55.218
<i>I saw
Mr. Mom four times.</i>

10:55.252 --> 10:58.805
(air blowing)

10:58.839 --> 11:02.008
(laughing):
That's not how you dry a baby.

11:02.042 --> 11:05.044
He has to do it
'cause his wife has the job.

11:05.079 --> 11:07.046
But motherhood's a job.

11:07.081 --> 11:09.082
M-Motherhood's
a job, too, though.

11:10.984 --> 11:12.101
Morning.

11:12.135 --> 11:13.186
What the hell?!
Where'd you come from?!

11:13.237 --> 11:14.654
Yeah, what are...
what are you doing

11:14.688 --> 11:16.155
out here in the middle
of nowhere?!

11:16.190 --> 11:17.323
Middle of nowhere?!

11:17.357 --> 11:18.858
No, I live right
over there.

11:18.892 --> 11:21.027
What?! A plane crashes
in your backyard,

11:21.061 --> 11:22.695
and you don't say anything?!

11:22.730 --> 11:24.097
This is Canada, eh?

11:24.131 --> 11:26.532
I thought it might be rude to
intrude on your plane crash.

11:26.567 --> 11:27.767
Well, this is
great news!

11:27.801 --> 11:28.935
We're saved!

11:28.969 --> 11:30.303
Yes, I suppose so.

11:30.337 --> 11:32.488
Listen, why don't we
head back to my place,

11:32.523 --> 11:35.358
grab a bite to eat and watch
the big curling match on TV?

11:35.392 --> 11:38.294
Canada is in the world
final against Mexico.

11:41.598 --> 11:46.068
No, no, no, no, no, no,
no, no, no, no, no, no!

11:47.104 --> 11:49.238
(cheering)

11:49.273 --> 11:53.392
(car horn
plays "La Cucaracha")

11:53.427 --> 11:55.445
Okay, well, we have
to grab our friend Peter.

11:55.479 --> 11:57.447
He just headed out
that way to get help.

11:57.481 --> 11:59.181
Oh. That's not good.

11:59.199 --> 12:00.633
There's nothing
in that direction

12:00.667 --> 12:02.618
but wilderness for
thousands of kilometers.

12:02.653 --> 12:03.970
What?! You're kidding.

12:04.004 --> 12:05.738
Oh, crap. Peter!

12:05.789 --> 12:07.857
Peter, come back! Peter!

12:07.891 --> 12:11.244
(garbled, electronic):
Peter! Peter!

12:11.278 --> 12:12.979
I think he's gone.

12:23.640 --> 12:26.592
I'm afraid he might not
have survived, Mrs. Griffin.

12:26.627 --> 12:28.427
We've been searching
these woods for weeks.

12:28.479 --> 12:30.062
We've got to keep looking.

12:30.097 --> 12:31.781
He's got to be out there
somewhere.

12:31.815 --> 12:33.349
Oh, my God,
we're in Canada.

12:33.383 --> 12:35.434
I get to use my
new passport.

12:35.486 --> 12:38.521
I hope my picture
looks okay.

12:38.555 --> 12:40.289
That's Liam Hemsworth.

12:40.324 --> 12:41.741
No, it's not. It's me.

12:41.775 --> 12:43.276
It's just a
really good angle.

12:43.327 --> 12:45.444
God, just look
for Peter. God.

12:47.998 --> 12:49.232
I'm sorry, Mrs. Griffin,

12:49.266 --> 12:51.634
there's been no sign
of your husband for two months.

12:51.668 --> 12:53.369
We're gonna have
to suspend our search.

12:53.403 --> 12:55.671
But there's got to be
something we can do!

12:55.706 --> 12:57.773
Well, the odds were always
against us.

12:57.808 --> 12:59.609
Most black men don't
possess the skills

12:59.643 --> 13:01.511
to survive out here
in the wilderness.

13:01.545 --> 13:03.212
My husband isn't black.

13:03.247 --> 13:04.447
Oh, boy.

13:04.481 --> 13:07.517
Well, then, we need
to regroup because...

13:07.551 --> 13:09.185
we haven't been looking.

13:09.219 --> 13:12.255
Wait! We may have a possible
lead on Peter Griffin!

13:12.289 --> 13:14.457
What?! Oh, my God,
where is he?!

13:14.491 --> 13:17.760
Well, we didn't find him,
but we found this.

13:19.263 --> 13:20.863
And there's some scribbling
on the back

13:20.898 --> 13:22.098
that says, "Mrs. G."

13:22.132 --> 13:25.651
We figured "G" has
to stand for "Griffin."

13:25.702 --> 13:27.870
No. That G isn't for "Griffin."

13:27.905 --> 13:29.906
It's for "Garrett."

13:29.940 --> 13:32.675
<i>This is Mrs. Garrett
from The Facts Of Life!</i>

13:32.709 --> 13:34.160
<i>And Diff'rent Strokes.</i>

13:34.211 --> 13:35.528
Why does everyone forget that?

13:35.562 --> 13:37.613
Kids, your father
made this.

13:37.648 --> 13:38.881
That's so weird.

13:38.916 --> 13:41.450
Dad gets lost in the woods,
and he makes a doll?

13:41.485 --> 13:42.718
Eh, it's not that strange.

13:42.753 --> 13:44.754
<i>It's kind of what Tom Hanks did
in Castaway.</i>

13:44.788 --> 13:46.789
(seagull screeching,
waves crashing)

13:49.977 --> 13:52.879
I'm sorry, Wilson!

13:52.913 --> 13:55.548
(sobbing)

14:00.287 --> 14:02.204
Right here is where
we found the doll.

14:02.239 --> 14:04.941
Okay, everybody fan out
and look for your father.

14:04.975 --> 14:06.308
Peter!
Peter!

14:06.326 --> 14:08.594
Dad!
Fat-ass!

14:08.629 --> 14:11.163
(rustling in brush)

14:13.467 --> 14:15.568
(gasps)
Peter?!

14:21.174 --> 14:22.074
(gasps)

14:22.109 --> 14:23.976
Oh, thank God!
We found you!

14:24.011 --> 14:25.745
Are you okay?!

14:25.779 --> 14:27.980
(grunting and whimpering)

14:28.015 --> 14:29.248
What the hell?!

14:29.283 --> 14:32.318
It looks like he's become
some kind of feral beast.

14:32.352 --> 14:33.586
Oh, my God!

14:33.620 --> 14:35.838
(whimpering and muttering)

14:35.872 --> 14:39.225
I can't live by
society's rules, either.

14:49.117 --> 14:50.884
(grunting and muttering)

14:50.918 --> 14:52.385
Mom, I still
don't get it.

14:52.420 --> 14:54.554
How could Dad turn
into some kind of feral creature

14:54.555 --> 14:56.323
just from two months
in the wilderness?

14:56.357 --> 14:57.558
Yeah,
it's weird.

14:57.592 --> 14:58.792
He doesn't
even talk.

14:58.827 --> 15:01.395
Well, he was living among
wild animals for two months,

15:01.429 --> 15:03.797
and you know how impressionable
your dad can be.

15:05.633 --> 15:08.435
Hmm.

15:09.470 --> 15:11.438
Peter, this
is your home.

15:11.472 --> 15:13.257
(grunting)

15:13.291 --> 15:15.192
This is the couch
where you sit.

15:15.226 --> 15:17.227
(sniffing)

15:20.281 --> 15:22.983
Oh, yes, that's
the television.

15:23.017 --> 15:25.402
Kids, I think he
recognizes the TV.

15:26.771 --> 15:29.139
We're all thinking it,
and he just does it.

15:29.174 --> 15:32.042
(sighs) You guys, this is gonna
be harder than we thought.

15:32.076 --> 15:34.678
I guess we're all just
gonna have to work together

15:34.712 --> 15:37.414
to reintroduce your father
to civilized society.

15:37.448 --> 15:39.049
Well, that's gonna be
a challenge.

15:39.083 --> 15:40.517
But it can't be any harder

15:40.552 --> 15:42.586
than folding
that thousand-mile-long sheet.

15:42.620 --> 15:44.489
All right, I got my corner.
Everybody ready?

15:44.523 --> 15:45.924
Ready. Chris?

15:45.958 --> 15:47.492
Ready. Meg?

15:54.967 --> 15:56.968
You know, long ago,
before this happened,

15:57.002 --> 16:00.705
your father did make some videos
in case he ever became feral.

16:00.739 --> 16:02.941
I guess
they're worth a shot.

16:02.975 --> 16:05.243
Hello, feral Peter.

16:05.277 --> 16:07.712
If you're listening to this,
you've probably become feral,

16:07.746 --> 16:10.982
and you're probably thinking of
a nice juicy rabbit right now.

16:11.016 --> 16:12.884
(muttering)

16:12.918 --> 16:14.652
But this isn't
who you really are, Peter.

16:14.687 --> 16:16.387
We got to get you back on track.

16:16.422 --> 16:17.555
Isn't that right, buddy?

16:17.590 --> 16:18.823
(grunting
and muttering)

16:18.857 --> 16:20.825
(gasps)
Look, it's working!

16:20.859 --> 16:22.327
Okay, good. So, feral Peter,

16:22.361 --> 16:25.230
why don't you put in tape number
two and let's get started.

16:30.869 --> 16:33.104
Don't listen to that Peter!
That Peter is a liar!

16:33.138 --> 16:35.039
Run! Be free!
They will enslave you!

16:35.074 --> 16:36.407
(grunting gibberish)

16:36.442 --> 16:37.408
(grunts gibberish)

16:37.443 --> 16:39.344
(grunting gibberish)

16:39.378 --> 16:40.411
(grunting gibberish loudly)

16:40.446 --> 16:43.014
(grunting gibberish)

16:45.050 --> 16:46.217
All right, now,
I've been trying

16:46.252 --> 16:47.685
to figure out something
we can do together,

16:47.720 --> 16:49.053
and I think
I've found it.

16:49.088 --> 16:50.255
We're gonna sing a song.

16:50.289 --> 16:52.023
Now, I'll start, and
when I point to you,

16:52.057 --> 16:53.958
you'll sing your part.
Ready?

16:53.993 --> 16:55.493
♪ War!
(grunts)

16:55.527 --> 16:58.263
♪ Yeah! What is
it good for? ♪

16:58.297 --> 16:59.964
♪ Absolutely nothing!

16:59.999 --> 17:01.666
Okay, all right,
good job.

17:01.700 --> 17:03.167
Now, let's try
something else.

17:03.202 --> 17:05.036
♪ I wanna get you home and ♪
♪ Ugh

17:05.070 --> 17:06.838
♪ Double up
♪ Ugh, ugh.

17:06.872 --> 17:08.506
Okay, I like where
this is going.

17:08.540 --> 17:09.807
Let's take it
from the top.

17:09.842 --> 17:13.077
♪ I like big butts
and I cannot lie... ♪

17:13.112 --> 17:15.079
(crickets chirping)

17:15.114 --> 17:17.015
(Peter mutters, thudding)

17:17.049 --> 17:18.950
(Peter gibbering in distance)

17:18.984 --> 17:20.084
(clattering and banging
in distance)

17:20.119 --> 17:22.687
Oh, damn it, he's in
the garbage again!

17:25.124 --> 17:26.257
Oh, my God!

17:26.292 --> 17:28.393
Come on! Get out
of the trash.

17:30.396 --> 17:32.363
Who threw out my sequin top?

17:32.398 --> 17:34.699
STEWIE:
You're 43! Accept it!

17:37.736 --> 17:39.704
(knocking on door)

17:40.739 --> 17:41.906
Hey, Lois.

17:41.940 --> 17:43.408
Oh, hi, Glenn.

17:43.442 --> 17:46.144
We just thought we'd come by
and see how Peter was doing.

17:46.178 --> 17:47.378
Yeah, has he gotten
any better?

17:47.413 --> 17:48.579
I'm afraid not.

17:48.614 --> 17:50.281
He's still only
speaking gibberish.

17:50.316 --> 17:52.316
Yeah, and we could live
without the semen throwing,

17:52.318 --> 17:53.451
I'll tell you that.

17:53.485 --> 17:55.186
I don't know
what else to do.

17:55.220 --> 17:57.522
We've tried everything
to bring the old Peter back,

17:57.556 --> 17:59.991
but we can't
break through to him.

18:00.025 --> 18:02.360
Lois, I think you've
done all you can.

18:02.394 --> 18:04.095
Hard as it is to admit,

18:04.129 --> 18:06.998
it might be time to let
Peter be where he belongs.

18:07.032 --> 18:08.249
What do you mean, Brian?

18:08.283 --> 18:11.052
We might have to release
Peter back into the wild.

18:11.086 --> 18:12.587
What?! We-we can't do that.

18:12.621 --> 18:14.922
We'll never
see him again!

18:14.957 --> 18:17.025
Eh, enough time passes,
you'll forget all about him.

18:17.059 --> 18:18.726
Just like
Inspector Gadget forgot

18:18.761 --> 18:20.428
about that one-night stand.

18:20.462 --> 18:22.096
Suzanne, we have to talk.

18:22.131 --> 18:23.364
Okay.

18:23.399 --> 18:25.566
Someone has made
contact with me.

18:25.601 --> 18:29.470
Someone from my past, and,
well, long story short,

18:29.505 --> 18:32.540
go, go, Guatemalan son.

18:35.577 --> 18:38.813
(speaking gibberish)

18:38.847 --> 18:41.983
Peter, we all
love you very much,

18:42.017 --> 18:44.285
but we think maybe
this is where you belong

18:44.319 --> 18:46.387
and where you'll be happy.

18:46.422 --> 18:47.955
This is barely
even a forest.

18:47.990 --> 18:50.058
I can see an Arby's
through the trees.

18:50.092 --> 18:51.526
(cries)

18:51.560 --> 18:52.794
You're free, Peter.

18:52.828 --> 18:54.896
Whatever adventures
lie ahead of you,

18:54.930 --> 18:58.299
know that my heart
will always be with you.

18:58.333 --> 19:01.402
Now, go! Go on!
Get out of here!

19:04.907 --> 19:06.407
(gibbering)

19:06.442 --> 19:09.093
I always knew, one day,
this would happen.

19:09.128 --> 19:12.730
We will never forget you,
but you're better off here.

19:12.765 --> 19:14.866
Why are we dragging this out?
It's 6:30.

19:14.900 --> 19:17.168
Go ahead, Peter.
Go on!

19:17.202 --> 19:18.970
Be with your own kind!

19:21.940 --> 19:23.708
We're all gonna
miss you, Dad!

19:23.742 --> 19:25.410
More than you know!

19:26.779 --> 19:29.747
(speaking gibberish)

19:29.782 --> 19:31.382
What?
My God, I...

19:31.417 --> 19:33.518
I think he's trying
to talk to you.

19:33.552 --> 19:34.719
(gasps)
Quick, Meg!

19:34.753 --> 19:35.920
Say something else!

19:35.954 --> 19:37.889
Well, okay, um...

19:37.923 --> 19:39.357
I love you, Dad,

19:39.391 --> 19:42.260
and no matter where you are,
you'll always be my father.

19:42.294 --> 19:44.562
(speaking gibberish)

19:44.596 --> 19:46.998
Shut... up...

19:47.032 --> 19:48.433
M-Meg.

19:48.467 --> 19:50.001
(all gasp)
I don't believe it!

19:50.035 --> 19:51.536
Peter, you talked!

19:51.570 --> 19:53.604
(garbled):
Shut up, Meg.

19:53.639 --> 19:55.840
Shut... up, Meg.

19:55.874 --> 19:58.543
Shut up... Meg.

19:58.577 --> 20:00.545
Shut up, Meg.

20:00.579 --> 20:01.746
Peter!
CHRIS: Dad!

20:01.780 --> 20:03.548
BRIAN: You're back!
MEG (flatly): Yay.

20:06.668 --> 20:08.636
Jim.

20:08.670 --> 20:11.405
Your name is Jim.

20:11.440 --> 20:13.341
Close enough, Peter!

20:13.375 --> 20:15.143
Come on, you guys,
let's go home!

20:15.177 --> 20:16.744
(overlapping chatter)

20:16.778 --> 20:17.912
Yeah!

20:17.946 --> 20:19.914
(indistinct chatter
and laughter)

20:19.948 --> 20:22.583
STEWIE: Is she...
is she letting him drive?

20:25.854 --> 20:28.022
Boy, I sure am happy
to be back.

20:28.056 --> 20:30.491
Yeah, Peter, we thought
we'd lost you for good.

20:30.526 --> 20:32.126
And luckily,
I don't know many words,

20:32.161 --> 20:34.695
so I was able to relearn them
all in a day.

20:34.730 --> 20:36.264
Don't ever leave
us again, Dad.

20:36.298 --> 20:37.932
Aw, sorry, Chris,
but this weekend,

20:37.966 --> 20:40.301
Quagmire's taking me
to a Canadian strip club.

20:40.335 --> 20:43.871
He said he's put together a very
special surprise, just for me.

20:43.906 --> 20:45.940
<i>MAN (French accent, over P.A.):
Mesdames et monsieurs,</i>

20:45.974 --> 20:48.609
all the way from America,
please welcome

20:48.644 --> 20:50.344
Miss Charlotte Rae!

20:50.379 --> 20:52.613
(applause and cheering)

20:52.648 --> 20:54.815
Mrs. Garrett!
Mrs. Garrett!

20:54.850 --> 20:57.151
Girls, girls, girls!

20:57.152 --> 21:58.152
Thank you for watching «Family Guy» on
www.FOX-fan.ru